WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.134 --> 00:00:16.606
Anne and Gilbert were sitting under an apple tree in the orchard behind Patty's Place.

2
00:00:16.607 --> 00:00:23.269
Anne looked as if she were going to cry. Gilbert's face was downcast, as he waited for her to speak.

3
00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:28.167
"I . . . can't be your wife," said Anne miserably.

4
00:00:28.168 --> 00:00:37.847
"Oh, Gilbert . . . you . . . you've spoiled everything!" She dared not look at him.

5
00:00:37.848 --> 00:00:41.745
"Don't you care for me at all?" Gilbert asked after a long pause.

6
00:00:41.746 --> 00:00:45.931
"No, not in that way," Anne said in a low tone.

7
00:00:45.932 --> 00:00:51.660
"I do care a great deal for you as a friend, but I don't love you, Gilbert."

8
00:00:51.661 --> 00:00:57.251
"But can't you give me some hope that you will . . . one day?" Gilbert asked.

9
00:00:57.252 --> 00:01:04.832
"No, I can't," said Anne desperately. "I can never, never love you in that way, Gilbert.

10
00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:11.579
I'm so sorry. Please don't speak about this again." Gilbert's face was pale.

11
00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:17.336
Anne shuddered and looked away. There was nothing romantic about this proposal.

12
00:01:17.337 --> 00:01:21.812
Would she ever forget the crushed look on Gilbert's face?

13
00:01:21.813 --> 00:01:25.376
"Is there anybody else?" Gilbert asked at last.

14
00:01:25.377 --> 00:01:33.704
"No," said Anne quickly. "I don't care for anyone like that—and I like you better than anybody else in the world.

15
00:01:33.705 --> 00:01:39.227
We must keep on being friends. I love being friends with you, Gilbert!"

16
00:01:39.228 --> 00:01:43.028
Gilbert gave a small bitter laugh.

17
00:01:43.029 --> 00:01:51.017
"Friends! Your friendship can't satisfy me, Anne. I want your love," Gilbert said firmly.

18
00:01:51.018 --> 00:01:56.374
"I'm sorry. Forgive me, Gilbert," was all Anne could say.

19
00:01:56.375 --> 00:01:59.713
"There isn't anything to forgive," he said quietly.

20
00:01:59.714 --> 00:02:02.843
"There have been times when I thought you did love me.

21
00:02:02.844 --> 00:02:07.174
I guess I've deceived myself, that's all. Good-bye, Anne."

22
00:02:07.175 --> 00:02:13.162
Gilbert walked slowly away, leaving Anne sitting in the orchard alone.

23
00:02:13.163 --> 00:02:19.444
As soon as Gilbert was out of sight, Anne ran inside, to her room, and lay on her bed, crying bitterly.

24
00:02:19.445 --> 00:02:23.370
She felt as if something very precious had gone out of her life.

25
00:02:23.371 --> 00:02:29.361
It was Gilbert's friendship, of course. Phil heard Anne's sobs and came in quickly.

26
00:02:29.362 --> 00:02:32.821
"What's the matter, sweetie?" asked Phil.

27
00:02:32.822 --> 00:02:38.171
Anne did not answer. She wished Phil were a thousand miles away.

28
00:02:38.172 --> 00:02:45.244
"I suppose you've gone and refused Gilbert Blythe!" Phil exclaimed. "You are an idiot, Anne Shirley!"

29
00:02:45.245 --> 00:02:50.869
"Is it idiotic to refuse to marry a man I don't love?" asked Anne coldly.

30
00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:54.423
"You don't know love when you see it," Phil insisted.

31
00:02:54.424 --> 00:03:00.650
"You think love is a storybook romance with a mysterious handsome stranger!"

32
00:03:00.651 --> 00:03:04.734
"Phil," pleaded Anne, "please leave me alone.

33
00:03:04.735 --> 00:03:09.406
My world has fallen apart. I want to rebuild it."

34
00:03:09.407 --> 00:03:14.797
"You may have to do that without Gilbert in it now," said Phil, going out the door.

35
00:03:14.798 --> 00:03:19.414
A world without any Gilbert in it! Anne couldn't consider it.

36
00:03:19.415 --> 00:03:24.488
Well, it was all Gilbert's fault! He had spoiled their friendship, Anne decided.

37
00:03:24.489 --> 00:03:28.605
She would just learn to live without it.

38
00:03:28.606 --> 00:03:34.124
The next day was a Saturday, and Phil insisted that Anne accompany her on a quick visit to Bolingbroke,

39
00:03:34.125 --> 00:03:38.371
which was Phil's hometown and the town where Anne was born.

40
00:03:38.372 --> 00:03:46.960
Phil knew that Anne was badly shaken by Gilbert's proposal and hoped that the distraction of a weekend trip would make her feel better.

41
00:03:46.961 --> 00:03:55.847
Anne enjoyed the dances, picnics, and dinners during her stay at Phil's lively family home, except in the moments when she thought about Gilbert.

42
00:03:55.848 --> 00:03:59.927
The happiest part of the weekend, though, was Anne's visit to her birthplace.

43
00:03:59.928 --> 00:04:09.641
In an out-of-the-way street, she saw the rundown little yellow house that she had so often dreamed about. Anne's eyes were filled with delight.

44
00:04:09.642 --> 00:04:15.872
She and Phil opened the gate, walked down the little path, and knocked on the front door.

45
00:04:15.873 --> 00:04:19.135
A very tall, thin, old lady answered.

46
00:04:19.136 --> 00:04:26.995
"Yes, the Shirleys lived here twenty years ago," she said in answer to Anne's question.

47
00:04:26.996 --> 00:04:31.427
"They rented the house when I used to live down the street.

48
00:04:31.428 --> 00:04:36.883
I remember them. They both died of fever at once.

49
00:04:36.884 --> 00:04:46.089
It was terribly sad because they left a baby; I guess it died long ago. It was a sickly thing."

50
00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:51.165
"It didn't die," said Anne, smiling. "I was that baby."

51
00:04:51.166 --> 00:05:01.915
"Really? I don't believe it! Why, you've grown," exclaimed the woman, as if she were surprised that Anne was not still a baby.

52
00:05:01.916 --> 00:05:08.408
"Oh, yes," she continued. "I see the resemblance now.

53
00:05:08.409 --> 00:05:18.275
Your father had red hair, but your eyes and your nose are like your mother's. They were both lovely people."

54
00:05:18.276 --> 00:05:22.537
"Could I have a look inside the house?" asked Anne eagerly.

55
00:05:22.538 --> 00:05:30.338
"Yes, you can look around if you like," the old woman said. "It won't take long."

56
00:05:30.339 --> 00:05:34.237
Anne went up the narrow stairs and into the little bedroom.

57
00:05:34.238 --> 00:05:39.441
So this was the room where she had been born . . . and where her mother had later died.

58
00:05:39.442 --> 00:05:43.847
Anne looked about her reverently; her eyes filled with tears.

59
00:05:43.848 --> 00:05:52.173
Anne went back downstairs, and the old lady met her in the hall. She held out a dusty little packet tied with faded blue ribbon.

60
00:05:52.174 --> 00:05:58.592
"Here's a bundle of old letters I found in that closet upstairs when I moved in.

61
00:05:58.593 --> 00:06:07.579
The name on the top letter is Bertha Willis, your mother's maiden name. I think you should take them."

62
00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:12.826
"Oh, thank you, thank you!" cried Anne, holding the packet tightly.

63
00:06:12.827 --> 00:06:16.048
"I don't have anything that belonged to my mother.

64
00:06:16.049 --> 00:06:20.064
I . . . I can never thank you enough."

65
00:06:20.065 --> 00:06:24.883
Phil and Anne left the little yellow house, and caught the train back to Halifax.

66
00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:28.835
Throughout the journey Anne fingered the faded ribbon on the packet,

67
00:06:28.836 --> 00:06:33.918
but she waited until she was home at Patty's Place to read the precious letters.

68
00:06:33.919 --> 00:06:39.922
There were not many of them, and some were written by her mother, others by her father.

69
00:06:39.923 --> 00:06:42.800
They both wrote of how much they loved the other.

70
00:06:42.801 --> 00:06:48.151
In the sweetest letter, Anne's mother wrote proudly about her new baby, saying,

71
00:06:48.152 --> 00:06:52.571
"I love her best when she's asleep and even more when she's awake."

72
00:06:52.572 --> 00:06:57.324
"This has been the most beautiful day of my life," Anne thought.

73
00:06:57.325 --> 00:07:08.632
"I've found my mother and father. Those letters have made them real. I'm not an orphan anymore."
